From ed529cc2cefc16f6274bda12b2ffc5cd79ddeda8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: atomozero <bernardi.andrea@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 07:52:56 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 28/42] bluetooth_server: a store for the bonds, their keys and
 the known devices

The paired devices were kept in one file of the user's settings, written
whole from the list of the devices the server met: the link keys with
the names, classes and services, readable by everyone, for whatever
adapter, and devices that never paired with them. The rules on when a
pairing is kept and when a key is forgotten were spread over the server.

BondStore keeps the bonds of one adapter and applies the rules of the
specification in one place: a key is kept when both sides of a simple
pairing asked for a bond (Dedicated or General Bonding), and for legacy
pairings; a debug key, or a pairing without a bond, serves the connection
only; a changed combination key keeps the kind it replaces; a key is
forgotten on "PIN or Key Missing", and on "Authentication Failure" only
on a link the device opened. It tells a listener of its changes, outside
its lock, and gives out copies only.

The keys are not in it but in a KeyVault, which the store only asks.
FileKeyVault keeps them in a file of their own, which only the owner may
read; the key store of the system can be another vault once it encrypts
what it keeps. DeviceCache keeps what is known of the devices, paired or
not, and lets those that are not go after a while.

Each adapter has a folder of its own, named after its address, with the
files "bonds", "devices" and "keys". They are written aside and put in
place; one that cannot be read is put aside as "<name>.broken", and a key
is never thrown away on reading. Fields a later version adds are kept.

Not used by the server yet.

Signed-off-by: atomozero <bernardi.andrea@gmail.com>
